ed  many to hope for a housing market crash  so that they can finally buy for a  reasonable price but even if there was a  housing crash it wouldn't be much easier  for californians to buy a house here's  the thing the median sale price across  the entire state is a whopping at 843  000  even if the housing market were to crash  30 percent the median sale price would  stand at 600 thousand dollars which is  still higher than the median sale price  before the pandemic started the median  household income in california currently  stands at 78 000  this means that the average house costs  over 10 times the average family's  annual income and even with the 30 crash  the average house would cost about seven  and a half times the average income this  has made homeownership completely  unattainable for the average californian  family and if you're a single person you  probably wouldn't even consider a house  to make things worse and it's not like  things are much better for renters for  families renting a three-bedroom house  will on average cost them three thousand  dollars per month or 36 000 per year  that's literally higher than the median  personal income in the us in 2020  and it's not just housing that's crazy  expensive either basically every facet  of daily life is significantly more  expensive take gym memberships for  example the national average for a gym  membership is 58  meanwhile the average gym membership in  l.a is 70  similarly in california the average  haircut for a woman is 69 dollars and  the average haircut for a man is 45  dollars the national average is 44  dollars for women and 28 dollars for men  these insane californian prices carry  over to clothing as well  the average pair of jeans cost 60 in  california while the national average is  nearly half of that at 34  and i don't even think we need to  discuss gas prices which currently  stands at over six dollars per gallon  aside from getting screwed over from a  cost of living perspective many  